messages:
jess: Heads up — hitting a weird duplicate-retry issue with the Kotlin SDK on Android 12 when ProGuard/R8 is enabled. App sends the same API request twice in quick succession and we see two chargeable tokens. :facepalm:

kai: ugh. does it only happen with auth flow? or any request?

jess: Seems tied to the authenticator callback. Repro in our sample app: enable minifyEnabled true, build release, call the SDK with a failing 401 then refresh token. First request completes, but OkHttp ends up issuing a second identical request.

olivia: can you paste the authenticator code? sometimes people accidentally create a new OkHttpClient inside the authenticator which triggers new interceptors / retry behavior.

jess: Here's our simplified Authenticator: ```kotlin
class TokenAuthenticator(val authClient: OkHttpClient, val tokenStore: TokenStore): Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    synchronized(this) {
      val newToken = refreshToken() // network call using authClient
      return response.request.newBuilder()
        .header("Authorization", "Bearer $newToken")
        .build()
    }
  }
}
```

sam: refreshToken() makes a blocking call on authClient — that pattern is fine but if authClient shares the same dispatcher/connection pool you can deadlock. Not sure why it duplicates though.

jess: The weird part — only reproducible in release build with R8. In debug everything is fine. Stacktrace shows two authenticate() calls back-to-back. One of them has a truncated stack with obfuscated method names.

olivia: maybe R8 removed some synchronization or inlined something. Any proguard/r8 rules for okhttp or retrofit in the SDK docs?

jess: We only had the basics: `-keep class okhttp3.** { *; }` but that didn't fix. Adding `-keep class com.redwood.sdk.** { *; }` *does* stop the duplicate retry. So it seems R8 was obfuscating some internal SDK helper used in the auth flow.

kai: Interesting. Could be reflection or a field name used by atomic operations. Do you use any internal package-scoped utilities that rely on exact method/field names?

jess: We do a tiny helper that checks `Request.tag()` to avoid replaying the same request during auth: `if (request.tag(RequestId::class.java) == null) attachRequestId()` — turns out R8 was stripping the RequestId class metadata in release build.

sam: That would explain it. If the tag check returns null both times, the second auth sees the request as untagged and tries again.

jess: Workaround for now: add `-keep class com.redwood.sdk.auth.RequestId { *; }` and include a note in the Android onboarding docs about ProGuard rules.

olivia: We should make the SDK robust: don’t rely on tag class names being preserved, or fallback to a header-based id. Also add explicit ProGuard rules in the setup guide.

kai: Agreed. For parity with Java SDK, ensure the Kotlin SDK sets a header `X-Redwood-Req-Id` on outgoing requests and use that for dedupe. Header survives R8.

jess: I'll open a tiny patch to add header fallback and add default proguard rules to the README/sample app.

sam: Also add a QA test: build release with minifyEnabled true, run sample app E2E to assert no duplicate requests when token refresh fires. We missed this in CI.

build-bot: CI job queued: android-release-e2e-minified -> #4821

jess: Thanks — one more note: observed on Android 12 and 13 devices but not emulator x86, seems device-dependent timing. Could be dispatch differences.

olivia: annotate the onboarding doc: "If you enable code shrinking for release builds, add the provided R8/ProGuard rules or use the header-based dedupe in SDK v1.2.4+."

kai: I'll follow up with a short devex blurb and a sample gradle snippet:
```gradle
android {
  buildTypes {
    release {
      minifyEnabled true
      proguardFiles getDefaultProguardFile('proguard-android-optimize.txt'), 'proguard-rules.pro'
    }
  }
}

// proguard-rules.pro
-keep class com.redwood.sdk.auth.RequestId { *; }
-keep class com.redwood.sdk.** { *; }
```

jess: Perfect. I'll attach the sample-app PR and the small README update. Tagging @olivia for docs and @sam for CI test. :thumbsup:

olivia: I'll add the rule to the quickstart and put a note in the SDK parity checklist so the Java SDK matches.

sam: once CI passes, let's push a patch release with the header fallback so existing users with R8 won't be stuck.

jess: Filed: https://redwood.internal/issue/RED-2301 — sample app fix + proguard docs + header fallback. Closing the loop here, thanks all.
